ials","k":"library","la":-37.645438,"lo":176.189441,"d":1237,"wifi":1},{"n":"New World","g":"essentials","k":"supermarket","la":-37.653809,"lo":176.198968,"d":2480,"ph":"+64 7 572 7080"},{"n":"Moturiki Island","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-37.633333,"lo":176.183333,"d":318,"ds":"Moturiki Island is a small island located just off Mount Maunganui beach, in the North Island of New Zealand. The island is connected to the beach by an artificial land bridge. NIWA maintains a tide meter on Moturiki Island.","wpu":"Moturiki Island","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Moturiki_Island%2C_Mount_Maunganui.jpg/960px-Moturiki_Island%2C_Mount_Maunganui.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Bay Oval","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-37.652778,"lo":176.190833,"d":2021,"ds":"Bay Oval is a cricket ground in Mount Maunganui, Tauranga in the Bay of Plenty area of New Zealand. The ground was built in Blake Park and opened in 2005. Bay Oval has hosted men's and women's international limited overs matches since 2014.","wpu":"Bay Oval","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Bay_Oval_2024.jpg/960px-Bay_Oval_2024.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Omanu","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-37.659757,"lo":176.214844,"d":3916,"ds":"Omanu is a beach and suburb in Tauranga, in the Bay of Plenty Region of New Zealand's North Island. The long, sandy beach is accessible via several public walkways through the dunes. It is a popular place to swim, surf and walk, and hosts surf lifesaving competitions.","wpu":"Omanu","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Omanu_SLSC_and_beach.jpg/960px-Omanu_SLSC_and_beach.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Otūmoetai","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-37.666667,"lo":176.15,"d":4423,"ds":"Otūmoetai is a suburb of Tauranga in the Bay of Plenty region of New Zealand. The Otūmoetai peninsula includes both the Otūmoetai suburb, and the neighbouring suburbs of Matua and Bellevue.","wpu":"Otūmoetai","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Ot%C5%ABmoetai_Beach.jpg/960px-Ot%C5%ABmoetai_Beach.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Baycourt Community and Arts Centre","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-37.6832,"lo":176.1676,"d":5399,"ds":"Baycourt Community and Arts Centre is a multi-purpose performing arts and theatre facility in Tauranga, New Zealand. It was designed by Warren and Mahoney, and has a main auditorium, the Addison Theatre, that seats 589.","wpu":"Baycourt Community and Arts Centre","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Baycourt_Community_and_Arts_Centre.jpg/960px-Baycourt_Community_and_Arts_Centre.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Matua, New Zealand","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-37.665376,"lo":176.131865,"d":5491,"ds":"Matua is a suburb of Tauranga, in the Bay of Plenty Region of New Zealand's North Island.","wpu":"Matua, New Zealand","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2f/Matua_Sunset_-_panoramio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Baypark Raceway","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-37.680556,"lo":176.234444,"d":6771,"ds":"Baypark Raceway was a 2.1348 km (1.3265 mi) permanent motor racing track that was located near Mount Maunganui in the Bay of Plenty Region, New Zealand. It was opened in December 1967 and had a relatively simple layout with only four corners. There was also a dirt oval track situated within the infield of the circuit.","wpu":"Baypark Raceway","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Baypark_Circuit_layout_%281967-1995%29.png/960px-Baypark_Circuit_layout_%281967-1995%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Matapihi","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-37.696607,"lo":176.191083,"d":6786,"ds":"Matapihi is a suburb and peninsula of Tauranga in the Bay of Plenty region of New Zealand's North Island, surrounded on most sides by the Tauranga Harbour. It is connected to Mount Maunganui in the northeast by a land bridge, and Maungatapu in the southwest by Maungatapu Bridge. State Highway 29A runs through the suburb along this diagonal.","wpu":"Matapihi","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Matapihi_railway_bridge.jpg/960px-Matapihi_railway_bridge.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Memorial Park, Tauranga","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-37.6975,"lo":176.165,"d":7000,"ds":"Memorial Park is a park located in Tauranga, New Zealand. Originally named Centennial Park after the centenary of the signing of the Treaty of Waitangi, it was renamed Memorial Park after the Second World War to qualify for a government war memorial subsidy. The park features cenotaphs inscribed with the names of the local men who died in the First and Second World Wars.","wpu":"Memorial Park, Tauranga","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Memorial_park.jpg/960px-Memorial_park.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Tauranga South","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-37.706,"lo":176.155,"d":8138,"ds":"Tauranga South is a suburb of Tauranga, on New Zealand's North Island. It is located south-east of Judea, north-east of Gate Pa and south-west of Tauranga Central. The suburb has a bowls club which hosts bowls players from across the country.","wpu":"Tauranga South","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Historic_Village_on_17th_Avenue_in_Tauranga.jpg/960px-Historic_Village_on_17th_Avenue_in_Tauranga.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Tauranga Harbour","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-37.633333,"lo":176.083333,"d":8693,"ds":"Tauranga Harbour is the natural tidal harbour that surrounds Tauranga CBD and the Mount Maunganui area of Tauranga, New Zealand, and which flows into the Pacific Ocean at Mount Maunganui. The harbour is effectively two flooded river systems separated from the Pacific Ocean by Matakana Island. The harbour is a large tidal estuary with an area of some 200-km2 and has a tidal range of up to 1.98m.","wpu":"Tauranga Harbour","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/be/Abaconda_ocean-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Wairoa River (Bay of Plenty)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-37.68643,"lo":176.09414,"d":9552,"ds":"The Wairoa River runs north into Tauranga Harbour at the western end of the Bay of Plenty in New Zealand's North Island.","wpu":"Wairoa River (Bay of Plenty)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Tauranga_Harbour_FL16232337_%28cropped%29.jpg/960px-Tauranga_Harbour_FL16232337_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Maungatapu, Tauranga","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-37.721844,"lo":176.174029,"d":9571,"ds":"Maungatapu is a suburb and peninsula of Tauranga in the Bay of Plenty region of New Zealand's North Island. It is north-east of Hairini and south-east of Matapihi. The Maungatapu Peninsula and Matapihi Peninsula are connected by the Maungatapu Bridge.","wpu":"Maungatapu, Tauranga","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Welcome_Bay_Link_shared_path_bridge_004.jpg/960px-Welcome_Bay_Link_shared_path_bridge_004.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Gate Pa","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-37.716,"lo":176.139,"d":9667,"ds":"Gate Pa or Gate Pā is a suburb of Tauranga, in the Bay of Plenty Region of New Zealand's North Island. It is the location of the Battle of Gate Pā in the 1864 Tauranga campaign of the New Zealand Wars.","wpu":"Gate Pa","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Gate_Pa_June_6th_2019.jpg/960px-Gate_Pa_June_6th_2019.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Motuhoa Island","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-37.6408,"lo":176.0689,"d":9973,"ds":"Motuhoa Island is in the western Bay of Plenty of New Zealand's North Island, in Tauranga Harbour, over 2 km (1.2 mi) long, up to 35 m (115 ft) high and about 1.25 km (0.78 mi) southeast of Ōmokoroa, with 5 km (3.1 mi) of low cliff, which on the north is up to 8 m (26 ft) high, with almost no vegetation. A boat ramp is near the east tip of the island, on the north shore.","wpu":"Motuhoa Island","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Motuhoa_Island_in_1943.jpg/960px-Motuhoa_Island_in_1943.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Tauranga (Māori pronunciation: [ˈtaʉɾaŋa] , Māori language for \"resting place\" or \"anchorage\") is a coastal city in the Bay of Plenty Region and the fifth-most populous city of New Zealand, with an urban population of 160,900 (June 2025), or roughly 3% of the national population. It was settled by Māori late in the 13th century and colonised by Europeans in the early 19th century. It was constituted as a city in 1963.","wpu":"Tauranga","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Mount_Maunganui_25.jpg/960px-Mount_Maunganui_25.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}